<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tel Dan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laish, Tell el-Qadi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tel Dan is an archaeological mound in northern Israel at the foot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-hermon">Mount Hermon</a>, beside one of the sources of the Jordan. It was the Canaanite city of Laish, mentioned in Egyptian texts and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> letters, which the Bible says the tribe of Dan conquered and renamed. Finds include a mud-brick city gate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> and the 9th-century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tel-dan-stele">Tel Dan stele</a> naming the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a>'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tel-dan.jpg" alt="Tel Dan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hanay · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tel_Dan_Canaanite_Gate_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
